test(core): FIX-130 the last bare-id log filter in TrailTest

`aFailedOrTooLargePersistRecordsTheRowMetadataOnlyAndCountsIt` filtered the
captured WARNINGs by the bare id substring "m-f" over message + getThrown()
and then asserted only that the result was non-empty. That is the trap FIX-54
hit once and FIX-121 twice: "m-w" is a prefix of "m-wait", and it was safe
here only because "m-f" happens to prefix no other fixture id in the case.

The filter now selects WARNINGs by Trail's logger name and maps getMessage()
only, each of the three failing bodies is matched by its own full BlobKey, and
the case asserts exactly one WARNING per failing key instead of non-emptiness.
A fourth assertion pins that the body which was made durable logs none.

Two review rounds were spent on one subtlety, and it is the reason the first
two attempts were wrong: Trail interpolates the cause into the message text,
so narrowing the captured string to getMessage() does not separate the two
channels. BodyTooLargeException's text carries the key, so the "large"
iteration still matched through its cause. The key is therefore pinned ahead
of the interpolated cause, with startsWith("message m-f: the body <key> "),
where only Trail's own literals and the key can occupy the matched region.

The per-body checks run through assertAll so every iteration is observable;
without it the mutant below aborted at the first body and the one iteration
whose independence was in question was never reached.

Checks: VERIFIED_PASS Tests run: 13, Failures: 0, Errors: 0, Skipped: 0 before
and after (agent/work/FIX-130-green5.log, -green6.log). Hand mutant naming the
message id instead of the key at Trail.java:177 fails all three iterations
separately (-mutant-key2.log): steps 2 and 4 on the per-key count, step 3 on
the prefix while its count assertion passes, which is exactly the independence
being proved. cmp shows Trail.java byte-identical to HEAD after the restore.

